    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-3233",
      "problem": "Find the $y$-intercepts of the circle $x^2+y^2+2x-2y= 8,$ shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14275039/assets/8eb9a6488191e8cf8a5b6107f3501e7d01938cf071e77b75890d570fc99cbf7a.png)",
      "worked_solution": "We must have $x = 0$ at the $y$-intercepts. \n\nSo, we need to substitute $x = 0$ into the equation of the circle and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-2y & =8 \\\\ (0)^{2}+{y}^{2}+2(0)-2y & =8 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-2y+1 & =8+1 \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =9 \\\\ y-1 & =\\pm 3 \\\\ y & =1\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercepts occur at  $(0,-2)$ and $(0,4).$",
